I'm pretty sure what the problem is, I have looked at the source and my
MSC runtime library reference, and find that MSC7 uses an epoch date of
Dec 31, 1899 00:00:00 as the zero point of the time() function, instead of
the traditional epoch of Jan 1, 1970 00:00:00.
This means that the constant of 722578680L in the phase_of_the_moon routine 
in hacklib.c is about 70 years too small for MSC7, in addition time_t is
unsigned long in MSC7, and the difference between the current time and 
the constant of 722578680L is now quite negative and probably makes the
return expression be zero most if not all the time.

I haven't had the time, or the resources to try to compile with changing the
constant to see if that fixes the problem.  Perhaps someone that has compiled
the MSC version could try it?  I looked for the phase_of_the_moon code in
3.0j, and there is a PC-specific routine for it instead of the generic
code in hacklib.c



